On 04/11/12 22:58, Scott Dorsey wrote:

> Replace them all arbitrarily without testing them.  Especially when you
> have a board that is full of little tantalum decoupling caps, it's often
> just faster and easier to replace them all than find the bad ones.  On
> top of which, if one is bad, more will be bad soon.  Be pre-emptive.
> Use modern 105'C types.
> --scott

It's the correct way to do it, but very time consuming and not cheap, 
with min
ordering quantities, especially if the boards have a load of different 
values.
It does require some skill to avoid damaging the boards as well, as the 
leads
are often folded over or t cropped at the ends.

The best way I have found is to bring the psu up on a variac, over a 
period of
30 minutes or so, starting at 20 volts, then up in 20 v steps over the 
time period.
Then, look at all the caps for signs of bulging and replace those only. 
Check all
the rails with a scope for excessive ripple ( = dried out or hi Z caps) 
and replace
those and you should be ok. Tants are usually ok if they survive the initial
power on and soak warmup, but you can find the odd shorted one by 
measuring the
voltage drop across the pcb or, assuming a stiff supply, checking for 
overheating...

Regards,

Chris



