ChrisQ  <meru@devnull.com> wrote:
>
>The best way I have found is to bring the psu up on a variac, over a 
>period of
>30 minutes or so, starting at 20 volts, then up in 20 v steps over the 
>time period.

As mentioned earlier, this only saves the caps on the primary side of the
power supply.  A modern SMPS will not fire up until it can do so at full
voltage, and even the Decmate II has a mostly-modern SMPS.

On older equipment with linear supplies this can do some good, though.

>Then, look at all the caps for signs of bulging and replace those only. 
>Check all
>the rails with a scope for excessive ripple ( = dried out or hi Z caps) 
>and replace
>those and you should be ok. Tants are usually ok if they survive the initial
>power on and soak warmup, but you can find the odd shorted one by 
>measuring the
>voltage drop across the pcb or, assuming a stiff supply, checking for 
>overheating...

Tantalums pretty much always fail into shorts when they fail, aluminum
electrolytics usually fail open (or just drop in value).

Unfortunately on high current stuff like 11/780 memory cards, when a tantalum
fails it can take out board traces as well.
--scott
