(then I wrote)
>> The tradition for 8 in floppies was that they came preformatted
>> from the factory, usually in 3740 format, but not always.

>> RX02 uses the same sector header (FM format) but writes the data
>> inside the block at double density. I am not sure what happens
>> if you try to read a block before writing it.

> The RX02 use a bit in the header to tell that it's RX02 formatted block. 

I had forgotten that one. In the usual system, the sector headers
are not rewritten after formatting. There is a write splice (gap)
after the header and before the data to give the drive time to
turn on the write circuitry.

OK, get out my MXV21 manual. It seems that the sector header is
the same, but the Data Address Mark is different. The DAM is rewritten
along with the data for each block. That is, the first byte written
after the gap. The normal FM data address mark is X'FB', the DEC 
modified data address mark is X'FD'. There are also deleted data 
address marks, X'F8' and X'F9', respectively.

Address marks have a non-standard clock pattern such that they can
be uniquely identified when reading. 

> So if you read before writing, it will be the same as after writing. 
> Reading will use the bit to determine in which way to interpret the 
> actual data. And in a normal 3740 formatted disk, that bit is always 0, 
> meaning RX01 format.

The usual PC double-density disk format uses the IBM MFM (which isn't
the same as the DEC MFM) for sector headers and data. Since DEC uses
FM headers and address marks, and since some DEC-MFM patterns look
like FM address marks, more modifications have to be done to avoid
those patterns. It is described in the MXV21 manual, which is
available on bitsavers.

-- glen



