As I remember, it would be the new disk, DY1: in your case, for both.

Having not done this for many years, it copies the boot block to the
block at the beginning where it is read by the boot logic. The actual
RT11FB.SYS file can be anywhere on the disk.

Also, if I remember, it writes into the boot block the block address for
RT11FB.SYS (so you have to use the right one, not DY0:).

On boot, the hardware reads the boot block in and starts executing it.
That, then, has to find and read more of RT11FB.SYS and start running
it. If you just do the BOOT command it is only the second step that
is done, as it can read the directory.

