Bob Vines  <bobvines00@gmail.com> wrote:
>I've got a DECmate II that hasn't been plugged in for at least 10
>years.  Should I attempt to reform the capacitors before turning it
>on?

It's a switching supply, so you COULD in theory reform the caps by supplying
DC to the 5V and 12V rails and bringing it up slowly.  However, you can't
just bring it up on a variac because the supply won't start producing power
until it is able to produce proper voltages.

>How about its VR201-A monitor?  Do I need to worry about "smoking" it
>too without reforming the capacitors first?

That you can bring up on a variac.  However, it if were mine, I'd just
shotgun all the caps, especially that 50V one on the sweep circuit.
If it's been in heavy use, all the caps are probably out of tolerance
by now.

My general philosophy about reforming caps is that if caps have become
deformed that building up a new oxide layer with a slow build-up will
really not be permanent.  In addition, modern capacitors (if you buy good
105'C ones and not crap) are far better than the originals.  So if any
caps are marginal, I want to make them all fail as quickly as possible so
I can replace them now, rather than have them become flaky some time in
the future.

>Does anyone know what a DECmate II Word Processing System would have
>cost new in ~1985?

Not at all, but it was far overpriced for what it did....
--scott

